WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:10.880
Good morning and welcome to the NCBI Minute:
An Updated PubMed is on its way!

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.779
Today I am here with Kathy Canese and Jessica
Chan from the PubMed team. My name is Marie

00:00:16.779 --> 00:00:21.029
Collins and I will be doing the talking but
Jessica will be doing the typing of the questions

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:25.939
pod. If we don't get to your questions we
have a lot of people on the line. If we don't

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:31.479
get to questions you can always reach out
to us and send feedback after the webinar.

00:00:31.479 --> 00:00:36.570
Today I want to talk about the new PubMed.
I'm going to start with the roadmap or timeline

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:42.050
because that seems to be the number one question
that we get from folks. I will talk about

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:48.230
the goals, the Agile development model, why
we built PubMed, a new PubMed and how we've

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.530
built a new PubMed. I will talk about the
features that have already been released in

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:58.250
PubMed Labs and features that are still to
come, the responsive design of the new PubMed.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.370
Then I have a couple of slides about communication
and planning for the transition to the new

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:04.370
PubMed

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:10.690
I am starting with the Mission Statement or
just overarching goal for the new PubMed.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:17.310
We are transforming PubMed into a modern hub
with a fast, reliable, and intuitive searh

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:23.310
that connects people to the world's leading
sources of biomedical information. And when

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.950
you read this statement I think every time
I read this statement I think this new PubMed

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.560
is really in line with of the goals of NIH,
the goals of NLM strategic plan for sure,

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.950
and it sort of captures of the world we all
work in every day here at NCBI. This is pretty

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:41.319
much what we do.

00:01:41.319 --> 00:01:46.979
The roadmap, if you are not familiar with
the new PubMed this will be completely new

00:01:46.979 --> 00:01:53.549
to you. If you have seen a previous presentation
or read a previous article about the new PubMed,

00:01:53.549 --> 00:02:00.779
this will be a shift for you. The plan as
it stands right now, which I think is a pretty

00:02:00.779 --> 00:02:06.990
solid plan is to have PubMed Labs, which is
running right now and you can see of the yellow

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:16.260
on the top bar. It's up and running but not
the current production PubMed. January2020

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.920
we plan to make PubMed Labs the default production
PubMed, so we're arriving at PubMed early

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:29.750
next year; you'll arrive at the new PubMed.
Current production PubMed at that time will

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:34.330
remain archived and available for several
months.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:39.930
I will talk briefly about the goals for the
new PubMed. Again, people who have seen other

00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.480
presentations, this may be a little repetitive
but I'm also hoping to reach people who were

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:52.200
not aware at all we were going to a new PubMed.
We are shifting PubMed to a modern cloud architecture

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.650
focusing on a high-quality search. The new
PubMed will be fast and reliable, at least

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:05.420
as fast and reliable as current production
PubMed. It will deliver a modern experience

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:10.690
and our overarching goal for this development
is we build a system that is beloved. People

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:17.340
love current PubMed. We really hope they are
going to love new PubMed when it launches.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:23.260
We've built this in an Agile development environment.
This means we have released a minimal viable

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.210
product within enough features for early users
and at the same time we established an environment

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:34.801
to capture feedback for future development.
We released new features frequently and we

00:03:34.801 --> 00:03:38.870
pay continuous attention to technical excellence
and the good design.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:45.500
Who did we talk to in building this new PubMed?
We talked clinicians; researchers; professors,

00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:50.790
librarians, journalists, grant writers, bio
curators, Health Advocates and PubMed team

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:56.819
members. Our research our usability testing
our interviews have been exhaustive and they

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:01.550
have been ongoing. In an Agile environment
you can continue to do the usability testing

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:06.890
throughout the lifecycle of the project. Just
two weeks ago we had usability experts at

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:13.270
the Clinical Center at NIH showing folks the
PubMed Lab on the mobile device and testing

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:19.370
the usability there, getting feedback that
will further will further inform our development.

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:24.290
This is a huge endeavor. It's a huge project.
It's a huge shift from the current production

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:31.720
PubMed moving into the cloud. It's not just
an interface refresh. However, what our users

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:37.190
will see is the interface refresh. I'm starting
here and the bulk of my presentation will

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:43.520
actually be on the features of this new PubMed.
I am going to start with what you will see

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.610
on this new interface when you are in PubMed
Labs if looking at a single citation, and

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:54.050
then I will go back to you run a research,
and you've results and all of the things you

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.940
can do with you can do with the full result
set.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:03.000
I have put the URL here if you would like
to open a Web browser and play along. You

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.340
can look at labs either for the first time
or again if you have been there before. I

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:09.550
first time or again if you have been there
before. I am going to start if you were to

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:14.550
run a search in PubMed Lab and look at a single
citation. I will show you some of the things

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.560
that you'll see there.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:22.020
Here I am looking at an abstract in PubMed
Labs. You can see we added this navigation

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.030
from within the single result so you can go
from one result to the next without going

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:32.190
back to your full results list.
If you hover over the previous or next button,

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:37.300
you will see little bit of details for the
citation that either comes before or after

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:43.180
this one. Also when looking at a single abstract
you'll see we have the full text links. You

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:50.840
have publisher links, the PMC link if article
is available in PMC. Now we have added the

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:56.290
library link out using outside tools. You
can see up to five library icons. If you are

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:01.370
affiliated with the hospital as well as a
university, for example you can display the

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:07.610
icons for all of the institution you are affiliated
with and take advantage of all of those subscriptions.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:08.610
We have a new cite buton that I"ll show you
a little bit later. We've added the cite but

00:06:08.610 --> 00:06:14.590
not was show you a little bit later. We've
added the Favorites button. You can share

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:20.360
to Twitter, Facebook or grab a perma link.
And we have page navigation. You can use the

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:26.620
navigation menu to jump right onto similar
articles or references or the link out menu,

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:31.230
or you can scroll through the abstract page
to see the rest of the information that is

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:33.290
below the abstract.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:40.210
Back to our results page. There is a lot going
on here. I am hoping today what you will see

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:46.470
of PubMed Labs will assure you that it is
feature-rich, and we will go through this,

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:52.150
each of these features, most of these features
in a little more detail. We have the results

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:56.870
by year graph. You can click on the arrows
to expand it. You can use the circles there

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:05.570
on the bottom to narrow or broaden your range.
We have the save, e-mail, and more actions

00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:12.210
menu. We have a BestMatch sort order that
you can easily toggle to most recent. You

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:17.639
can change your view from the summary view
that you see in front of you on the screen

00:07:17.639 --> 00:07:24.020
to the full abstract view. You can see here
